Z||000000||sqoach||01-01-2001||12:36 AM||||don't know if it's the gauge or sensor or what, but my temp gauge shows that my car runs cold. the highest i've seen it get is the second mark. it runs just fine. the fan is not on when i drive it. i don't think it could keep it that cold anyway. the radiator cap is new (16 psi locking cap) new 195 stat. i kept the ring off of it cuz it stretched out when i took it off when burping the system. this is very strange because my car has been overheating as some of you know, until about a month ago. it was after i purchased the new stat and cap that it has been like this. please help! thanks.<P>------------------<BR>sQoaCh<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<BR>        <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mysig_2.JPG"> ||||reg||4
Z||000001||maninblack||01-01-2001||02:02 AM||||If the car is running fine, i suggest to leave it alon, as they always said "dont mess<BR>it, if its not broken.<BR>happy new yr||||reg||1
Z||000002||Hank is Here||01-01-2001||11:14 AM||||Could you have a bad sending unit?<BR>The unit is on the 4 cyl is on the drivers side top front of the head.  Take off the wires connected to it.  Make a jumper between the two and ground it out.  Your gauage should jump to the top and idiot light come on.  This is just s test.  <P>Mark Johnson||||reg||1
Z||000003||DaveL||01-01-2001||11:34 AM||||Put the ring back on it, it'll allow coolant to bypass the thermostat. As far as the guage goes, if you put in a new thermostat and cap, I'd think the guages were off. Fiero's apparently need to be kept cool, but not too cool!<BR>Happy Fieroing||||reg||1
Z||000004||sqoach||01-02-2001||01:31 AM||||mark, what wire is the ground wire on the connector to the sending unit? thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000005||FieroLisa||01-02-2001||01:54 AM||||my '86 4 cyl runs dead cold 99% of the time.(just below first mark) At daytona when it was hot and I was in stop and go traffic, temp shot up and the fan cooled it right down. You said you replaced something, so I wouldn't worry about it too much. Then again, I figure if I don't worry about things, I don't have to worry about repair costs  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I'm sure testing it would be a good idea as mentioned above.<P>------------------<BR>Lisa<BR>86 gold 4 speed GT<BR>86 gold 5 speed SC (For Sale)<BR>Columbus, OH/Greensboro NC<BR>AOL IM: FieroLisa<BR>*As Always, Updated Website*<A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Standard||01-02-2001||05:40 AM||||After I flushed my coolant and put in a 180* 'stat, my GT ran nice and cool, usually hovering around the second mark.  I've heard so many horror stories about the cooling that I couldn't believe it would run that well, but it does.  <P>As for your car, I would check the sensors first, and definatly put the O-ring back on the thermostat.  I have heard of some thermostat's being bad right out of the package, I would go buy another one just to check.  I had a 195* stat that I thought was good, tested it in a pan of boiling water and it opened up when it should, but my car never warmed up with it in.  The spring was too weak to hold it shut against the coolant flow..||||reg||1
